Fix, Windows: a crash when the size of the process cannot be asked for.

WTF::memoryFootprint() asks Windows for the pages of the process with a
small buffer first, and if that fails takes from the buffer how many
entries there are and allocates room for them. The number is there only
when the call failed because the buffer was too small. When it failed for
another reason the buffer was never written, the number is whatever was on
the stack, and the allocation of that many entries ends the process.

Under Wine the call always fails so (it is not implemented), and every
process of the engine died within half a minute of starting, when the
memory monitor first measured. The buffer now starts out zeroed, and the
reason for the failure is looked at before the number is, as the code does
for the calls after the first.

